"type":"text"},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"{v, u}"},{"text":".","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"Vertices are kept in dense slots, with a dictionary from vertex to slot. Edges are kept in an"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"array, so an edge’s position in "},{"code":"edges","type":"codeVoice"},{"type":"text","text":" is an "},{"code":"Int","type":"codeVoice"},{"type":"text","text":" in "},{"code":"0..<edgeCount","type":"codeVoice"},{"type":"text","text":". Each slot has a row of"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"the slots of its neighbors and a parallel row of the positions of its incident edges; each edge"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"is in both its endpoints’ rows, and a self-loop twice in its vertex’s row (as Boost stores it)."},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"The rows share one array per kind, so the graph makes a constant number of allocations however"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"many vertices it has. A dictionary from unordered slot pair to position answers"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"code":"contains(edge:)","type":"codeVoice"},{"type":"text","text":" in O(1)."}]},{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"Removing an edge is O(1): the last edge moves into its position, and the last entry of each"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"row into the hole. Removing a vertex costs O(degree) and moves the last slot into its place. So"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"vertex order, edge order and edge positions are unspecified and may change after a removal."},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"Each edge keeps the orientation it was first inserted with."}]}]}],"hierarchy":{"paths":[["doc:\/\/AdjacencyListModule\/documentation\/AdjacencyListModule"]]},"metadata":{"modules":[{"name":"AdjacencyListModule"}],"externalID":"s:19AdjacencyListModule010UndirectedaB0V",